\par }\pard {\f5 \tab The clock for the local oscillator is derived from the 14.318 MHz clock in the computer. This
 clock is applied to the DDS (Direct Digital Synthesizer), which is computer programmed to output from 2926.83 KHz to 5853.66 KHz. The output of the DDS is multiplied by 41 in the PLL to produce an output in the 120 to 240 MHz range (LO OUTPUT).  This out
put is divided by 2^n where n ranges from 2 to  6 to derive an output from 1.8 to 120 MHz. A digital network is used to convert this output into two quadrature outputs at one quarter of the  frequency (450KHz to 30MHz).  These quadrature outputs are atten
uated and applied to the quadrature mixers.
